responses1=[
('Sections 1 and 5','We have reframed the contribution as a transparent application-level comparison, rather than a new Bayesian method. The Introduction now includes Reichenbach et al. (2018) and Merghadi et al. (2020), explains inventory, pseudo-absence and mapping-unit uncertainty, and compares the regional validation objectives more specifically. We corrected Chowdhury et al. to the 2024 journal year (online publication in 2023) and removed the unsupported claim that it used random k-fold validation instead of its reported random 80/20 split. We explicitly recognize prior bivariate uncertainty mapping and avoid claiming it is unique to Bayesian models. The resolution details retained in the comparison are limited to those verified from source text.'),
('Section 2.1','We added the regional weathered shale/sandstone setting and explained how road proximity, LULC and NDVI can relate to excavation, vegetation removal and drainage change. These are identified as indirect proxies. Site-specific shear strength, bedding, cut geometry, building load and drainage alteration were not measured, so we do not imply those processes were directly represented or causally established.'),
('Section 2.2; Section 5','The modeling table does not retain failure-type, size or trigger fields. We now state this explicitly and explain that mechanism-specific modeling and a type/size breakdown cannot be reconstructed from the available records. We no longer assert that the sample is a verified homogeneous set. Potential mixing of shallow slides and cut-slope failures is described as a limitation, not as a documented count of either mechanism.'),
('Section 2.2; Table S9 equivalent audit in Section S8','We audited the actual input and document the reproducible step: 376 rows become 369 after removing seven exactly duplicated rows. The retained coordinates contain no exact duplicate pairs. We withdraw unsupported statements about 10 m event consolidation and systematic imagery verification. Event dates, source identifiers, positional errors, field checks and completeness records are absent from the analytical table, so those checks cannot be certified from it. We identify this as an unresolved provenance limitation rather than inventing a quality-control history. The reported 2001–2022 compilation period is explicitly qualified.'),
('Sections 2.3, 3.6 and 4.3; Table S6','We distinguish native resolution from the nominal target grid and state that resampling does not create new spatial information. Source resampling commands and raster metadata are unavailable, so we cannot certify a per-layer resampling history. We also found fractional LULC and soil codes and removed the incorrect one-hot-encoding claim. As an empirical sensitivity check, we refitted all four models without rainfall, without soil and without both. For example, removing rainfall increases GPC AUC to 0.848 but increases ECE to 0.118. These results qualify the coarse-predictor interpretation; they do not repair missing source provenance or prove fine-scale map reliability.'),
('Sections 1, 3.4, 4.1 and 4.2','Bayesian eligibility is now stated before results. RF remains the strongest overall benchmark, including its superior Brier score and log loss. We clarify the original rank weights and acknowledge holdout reuse in that score. A training-only ranking sensitivity check retains GPC as the stronger Bayesian candidate. We no longer use one ECE value to claim general superiority. False-negative comparisons now include every model: GPC 8/31, BLR 11/31, RF 7/31 and regularized logistic regression 7/31. RF recalibration was not implemented, and the manuscript does not claim equivalence to such a benchmark.'),
('Sections 3.5 and 4.4; Table S7; Figure 5','We provide numerical cut points and compare tertiles with Jenks breaks and fixed thirds. Susceptibility classifications agree with Jenks at 93.2% of sampled points, while uncertainty classifications agree at 74.3%. We removed the unsupported blanket assertion that both distributions are right-skewed and the claim of balanced joint spatial areas. The classes are relative sample categories, not hazard levels or management thresholds. The revised display uses corrected latent uncertainty at observed points because the archive cannot support verified continuous raster inference.'),
('Sections 3.6 and 4.4; Section S6; Figure S3','We regenerated Kernel SHAP for the GPC trained on 295 records, with 30 training background rows and all 74 holdout records explained. The output scale is positive-class log-odds, nsamples is 150, and seed is 42. The background and attribution array are supplied. No conditional dependence-aware SHAP method was used; this limitation is explicit. We removed causal language, claims about physical mechanism validation and unsupported assertions of stable importance across all validation folds.'),
('Sections 2.3 and 5','We distinguish static rainfall background from storm triggers and discuss event dates, antecedent rainfall and threshold validation. A regional event-based reference, Ahmed et al. (2018), is included. The archived CHIRPS aggregation years and product version are missing; we state this unresolved limitation rather than inventing a temporal window. Daily CHIRPS exists, but the analyzed table lacks a documented event linkage. The paper therefore makes no intensity–duration, early-warning or temporal hazard claim.')]
responses2=[
('Section 1','We now identify the contribution as an auditable empirical comparison of spatial validation, calibration stability and conditional uncertainty definitions. We explicitly recognize earlier uncertainty-aware and bivariate approaches. Claims of a new Bayesian algorithm, universal methodological novelty or an exclusively Bayesian ability to communicate uncertainty have been removed.'),
('Sections 2.2, 3.6 and 4.3; Table S5','The negative count is now exactly 214. Auditing the table contradicted the draft’s 500 m and below-10° claims: 71 negatives are within 500 m and 27 recorded slopes are at least 10°. We withdraw those claims. We performed three controlled negative-subset runs, each retaining all 124 training positives, 136 of 171 training negatives and an unchanged 74-record holdout. RF’s AUC lead persists, but MCC rankings change and GPC ECE increases. The experiment is explicitly conditional on the original negative pool and does not replace independent resampling from a surveyed candidate region.'),
('Sections 2.3 and 4.3; Table S6','We explicitly distinguish 30 m nominal alignment from the 0.05° CHIRPS and 250 m soil information supports. The predictor-ablation analysis provides empirical evidence that these variables affect metrics and probability behavior. We also disclose fractional categorical codes and the lack of resampling provenance. We do not interpret interpolation as additional information.'),
('Section 3.2; Figure S1; Tables S1 and S2','The code audit found nine clusters on the 295-location training subset, from the rule floor(n/30) capped at ten. We reproduce those assignments and metrics, show their spatial distribution and report every fold’s class counts and cluster point extents. A separate all-369-point ten-cluster illustration is also supplied, clearly labeled as distinct from validation. The 74-point holdout is random, with a minimum 4.52 m distance to training; it is not spatially independent. We no longer claim a fixed block size, a geographic buffer or guaranteed independence.'),
('Sections 1 and 3.4; Section 4.2','Bayesian deployment eligibility is now explicit before the results, while RF retains its overall benchmark lead. We disclose that original weighted ranking reused the holdout. The new training-only ranking sensitivity check selects GPC among Bayesian candidates without relying on the holdout criteria, but is described as a revision sensitivity check rather than a prespecified rule. We did not add a calibrated-RF benchmark and make no claim that an uncalibrated RF cannot be improved or equipped with other uncertainty methods.'),
('Section 3.5; Section S5; Figures 4 and 5','We found and corrected a substantive implementation issue: the saved uncertainty export equals 1 − 2|p − 0.5| rather than latent variance. We now compute latent moments directly, transform them by Gauss–Hermite quadrature to obtain probability-scale posterior variance and expected Bernoulli variance, and verify their sum against total Bernoulli predictive variance. We report the exact standard-deviation normalizer and class thresholds. Conditional epistemic and aleatory terms are distinguished, and the optimized hyperparameters and white-noise kernel are acknowledged. The original continuous posterior-uncertainty interpretation is withdrawn.'),
('Sections 3.3 and 4.2; Figure 3; Tables S3 and S4','We report 2,000 paired bootstrap resamples, 95% percentile intervals, an unsmoothed ten-bin reliability diagram with counts, and five/ten/fifteen-bin sensitivity. The GPC-minus-RF ECE interval spans zero. We also explain the small-sample upward bootstrap bias, including why the GPC point estimate is below its percentile interval. RF’s Brier score and log loss remain better. Claims that ten bins ensure low variance or that Bayesian status guarantees calibration have been removed.'),
('Abstract; Sections 1, 5 and 6','Terminology now consistently describes static susceptibility. Hazard is used only to explain the distinction requiring temporal information, or within bibliographic titles; risk is not presented as an estimated study output. We removed hazard-hotspot, forecast and operational safety claims that the data do not support.')]
